1
00:00:00,395 --> 00:00:03,462
Institutional desks owe
each other in tangled rings.

2
00:00:03,482 --> 00:00:08,988
Settle every debt one by one, and
capital stays locked while gas explodes.

3
00:00:09,283 --> 00:00:15,614
At 44 desks, our hardened Solidity
reference burns 16.65 million gas.

4
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:21,501
StylusClear rebuilds the clearing core
in no_std Rust on Arbitrum Stylus.

5
00:00:21,521 --> 00:00:28,875
The same batch models at 2.92M: 82.5%
less gas, while netting away up to 94% of

6
00:00:28,895 --> 00:00:30,559
the cash that has to move.

7
00:00:32,345 --> 00:00:33,979
Scenario one.

8
00:00:33,999 --> 00:00:37,748
Four trading desks owe $13,200 in a loop.

9
00:00:37,919 --> 00:00:42,283
A canonical 2-origin BFS finds
both cycles and nets them out.

10
00:00:42,303 --> 00:00:47,152
Only $1,050 still has to
move: 92% less liquidity,

11
00:00:47,172 --> 00:00:52,512
plus a 2 WETH batch-auction
fill at one uniform price.

12
00:00:52,895 --> 00:00:55,676
Now push a quote 26% outside the oracle band.

13
00:00:55,696 --> 00:00:59,153
The fail-closed guard reverts
with LimitPriceViolated,

14
00:00:59,173 --> 00:01:01,265
and the entire batch rolls back.

15
00:01:01,285 --> 00:01:03,429
Nothing settles halfway.

16
00:01:03,812 --> 00:01:10,296
On Robinhood Chain Testnet, the same
engine nets USDG against tokenized NVDAx:

17
00:01:10,316 --> 00:01:12,177
$24,000 gross becomes $1,400.

18
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:17,881
And a dust-collateral sweep means an
insolvent account can always be closed.

19
00:01:21,357 --> 00:01:23,381
Shipping is engineered too.

20
00:01:23,401 --> 00:01:31,266
The engine compiles to plain
WASM 1.0: 24,518 B after Brotli,

21
00:01:31,286 --> 00:01:36,331
54 B under the classic 24 KiB cap with
its header, and zero float opcodes.

22
00:01:36,626 --> 00:01:41,238
One atomic factory deploys, activates
and initializes it in a single

23
00:01:41,258 --> 00:01:46,814
transaction, and all 37 methods share
Solidity-identical reentrancy guards.

24
00:01:48,345 --> 00:01:50,797
This is the real workbench in Chromium.

25
00:01:50,817 --> 00:01:54,803
Each preset re-runs the clearing and
margin math right in the browser,

26
00:01:54,823 --> 00:01:57,331
and the oracle guard blocks the bad quote.

27
00:01:57,552 --> 00:02:02,717
Drag the mesh to 64 desks, and the
BFS still answers in milliseconds.

28
00:02:03,012 --> 00:02:07,425
On the right, the actual 5-stage judge
gates on this machine: Rust, Foundry,

29
00:02:07,445 --> 00:02:10,335
Z3 and the workbench seam, all green.

30
00:02:10,355 --> 00:02:15,510
Testnet manifests stay empty
until broadcast. No fake hashes.

31
00:02:20,345 --> 00:02:22,759
The benchmark is deliberately honest.

32
00:02:22,779 --> 00:02:27,942
Solidity runs on cold storage slots,
and the Stylus model prices every host

33
00:02:27,962 --> 00:02:30,724
call, including 20k-gas cold nonce writes.

34
00:02:30,969 --> 00:02:37,177
Savings grow with scale: 41% on
small batches, 82.5% at stress,

35
00:02:37,197 --> 00:02:40,812
all under Arbitrum's 32M-gas block cap.

36
00:02:41,107 --> 00:02:46,941
Six Z3 theorems are proved, and 100
fuzzed batches match the compiled Rust

37
00:02:46,961 --> 00:02:48,414
binary bit for bit.

38
00:02:48,759 --> 00:02:49,508
StylusClear.

39
00:02:49,528 --> 00:02:51,650
Clear more, move less.
